And there is a huge gulf between uh the the kinds of things that we're asking an LOM to do in terms of language or code, and actually uh and I mean you can design, which is great, but when you forecast that extreme deserves scrutiny of both the evidence and the language.

44
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:44.960
Planet Money's two indicators looks at single stock leveraged ETFs tied to South Korea's AI memory chip boom.

45
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.000
Futures and leverage multiply the upside.

46
00:04:48.000 --> 00:05:04.160
And when the chip trade reverses, the losses these look just like a normal ETF on the outside, except there's extra leverage, futures, and various other financial tricks on the inside to multiply your returns.

47
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.240
What could possibly go wrong?

48
00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:12.960
Yeah, there's a downside is that if the ETF loses value, the losses are also magnified.

49
00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.800
Now, these investments have been legal in the US since 2022.

50
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.200
South Korea wanted to keep up, so investors cut money in its stock market.

51
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:30.520
These ETFs became more popular in Korea as the value of those semiconductor companies grew, making up 20% of trading on the South Korean exchange on some days.

52
00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.760
I call them weapons of self-destruction.

53
00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.840
I don't I don't know why regulators uh approve these things.

54
00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.520
His pessimistic view seems to hold in Korea at least.

55
00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:46.120
The value of its stock market plummeted 40% at one point.

56
00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:52.360
The companies still estimate booming demand for their memory chips, but investors got a little less excited.

57
00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:57.240
The leverage bets started to unwind, and margin traders had to sell their investments.

58
00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:46.680
The majority of these accounts belong to people under the age of 35, according to Citibank.

59
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:52.200
Younger people felt confident taking on a lot of risk and maybe had less experience in financial markets.

60
00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:53.480
That's who got hurt.

61
00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.040
Now, is there a lesson for the United States?

62
00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.440
As we've discussed.

63
00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.600
Can conceal a very different risk profile.

64
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:14.000
On Invest Like the Best, veteran investor and writer Michael Moritz recounts how his two grandfathers responded differently to the rise of Nazi Germany.

65
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:22.560
It's a personal story about judgment under pressure and the consequences of recognizing danger too late.

66
00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.920
Maybe tell that story and explain what you learned from.

67
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:34.480
The two individuals, Patrick, that you referred to were my grandfathers, my maternal grandfather and my paternal grandfather.

68
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.360
I'll start with my paternal grandfather, who was a civil servant.

69
00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:46.560
He was a sort of lower-level judge in the judiciary in Bavaria province in southern Germany.

70
00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.400
He had fought in World War One.

71
00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:56.080
He had somehow or other survived the horror of Verdun.

72
00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:07.600
He had been given his iron cross, and he was, I think, a great believer in the German state, in the security that the state offered.

73
00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.960
And then much later in 1933, he lost his job.

74
00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.640
This is my assessment of the situation because he was a civil servant.

75
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:33.840
He was by nature conservative, by nature somewhat cautious, but also had this great belief in the state and the endurance of the state and the durability of the state, and that eventually the storm would blow over.

76
00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:39.680
He also had a pension, and the pension was very important to him as a means for survival.

77
00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:43.040
It was a combination of all of those things.

78
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:52.560
He wasn't oblivious, obviously, to what was happening around him, but it did make him more reluctant to leave Germany, I think, until it was too late.

79
00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:57.840
And people forget, even if somebody had decided to leave Germany, there are all sorts of obstacles in the way.

80
00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.000
He had to find a place elsewhere that would accept you.

81
00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:08.200
You had to pay big taxes to the Nazi government in order to be able to secure your exit visas.

82
00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.960
So there were all these sorts of obstacles.

83
00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.360
So that was my paternal grandfather.

84
00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:18.520
And he and his wife eventually were shipped off east and disappeared from history.

85
00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:22.120
My maternal grandfather was cut from a different cloth.

86
00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:25.480
I was too callous as a youth to really understand this.

87
00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:28.120
But he died when I was about 16.

88
00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:36.520
He had been a cattle trader, which was one of the occupations that Jews were really permitted to do in Germany.

89
00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:42.200
But he was almost blind as a young man and completely blind by the time that I met him.

90
00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:53.080
But somehow or other had been able to carve out a comfortable living for himself and his family by trading in cattle in a rural village in northwestern Germany.

91
00:09:53.080 --> 00:10:11.240
My assessment of him in retrospect was that he was more street-wise, he was more entrepreneurial, wasn't burdened by the need to remain and collect a state pension because he had always been able to sustain himself and look out for his future.

92
00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:15.320
And he got out with his wife, my grandmother.

93
00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.440
But they got out by the skin of their teeth.

94
00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:23.160
They got out 72 hours before war broke out.

95
00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.280
72 hours later, they too would have perished.

96
00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:27.960
What do you take from that?

97
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.080
What is the big lesson from that instinct?

98
00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:36.840
How does that instinct imprint it on you and on the people you are?

99
00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:39.800
That's today's Daily Rundown.

100
00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:41.080
Thanks for listening.

101
00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.920
We'll be back tomorrow.
